# Rank 1025 / CVE-2024-29857: importing an EC certificate with crafted F2m
# parameters burns CPU in Bouncy Castle. The certificate's explicit curve
# parameters reach X9Curve's parse constructor, where
# int m = ((DerInteger)parameters[0]).IntValueExact feeds
# new F2mCurve(m, k1, k2, k3, ...), and the binary-field curve degree drives
# field arithmetic with no upper limit, so a large m makes curve evaluation
# run far longer than any real key requires (CWE-400, CWE-125 also
# assigned). The fixed revision caps the degree inside BuildField, before
# the field is constructed:
#
#   int maxM = ImplGetInteger("Org.BouncyCastle.EC.F2m_MaxSize", 1142); // 2 * 571
#   if (m > maxM)
#       throw new ArgumentException("F2m m value out of range");
#
# ATTENTION, precision cost, re-measured. Nothing focused fires either side:
# scanning crypto/src at both revisions of the fix reports the same 1815
# findings, and ECCurve.cs alone reports the same four VYQL-NUM-005 rows and
# one VYQL-CFG-014 row, none concerning the degree. Both DOS rules this CVE
# invites are measured here. VYQL-DOS-006 (code.LengthDerivedAllocation)
# describes a parsed length controlling an allocation, while this cost is
# computation time and nothing is allocated from m. VYQL-DOS-042
# (code.UnboundedResourceOperation) names the right weakness class but the
# construct class it would need -- a caller-supplied numeric dimension
# requiring a ceiling -- cannot be demanded generically: methods and
# constructors taking an integer dimension such as m, n, size, len, bits or
# degree number 243 in crypto/src/math and 336 across crypto/src, and
# exactly one of them needed the ceiling this fix added. Narrowing to the
# curve constructors does not rescue it: new F2mCurve( appears at 36 sites
# in crypto/src, 19 with a variable first argument, and 18 of those 19 are
# named-curve tables whose m is a locally assigned compile-time constant --
# structurally indistinguishable from the parsed degree without vocabulary
# that already knows which dimension needs bounding.
#
# The judgement is also not structural. Knowing that the degree drives
# superlinear field arithmetic, and that 1142 is the sane ceiling because
# the largest standard binary curve is 571 bits, is cryptographic domain
# knowledge rather than anything the shape of the code carries. And the
# remediation is centralised in the callee: the attacker-controlled flow
# site (X9Curve.cs, ((DerInteger)parameters[0]).IntValueExact ->
# new F2mCurve(m, k1, k2, k3, ...)) is byte-identical across the fix, so a
# binding at the flow site cannot discriminate, and the only in-file signal
# the fix leaves in ECCurve.cs is its own check -- a negation keyed there
# encodes the fixing commit's layout rather than a documented control.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-precision-cost

test "rank1025: binary field built from a degree with no upper bound"
  lang csharp
  file crypto/src/math/ec/ECCurve.cs
  reject VYQL-DOS-006
  reject VYQL-DOS-042
  code
  ```
  public abstract class AbstractF2mCurve
      : ECCurve
  {
      private static IFiniteField BuildField(int m, int k1, int k2, int k3)
      {
          int[] exponents = (k2 | k3) == 0
              ? new int[]{ 0, k1, m }
              : new int[]{ 0, k1, k2, k3, m };

          return FiniteFields.GetBinaryExtensionField(exponents);
      }

      protected AbstractF2mCurve(int m, int k1, int k2, int k3)
          : base(BuildField(m, k1, k2, k3))
      {
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1025: fix rejects a degree above the configured maximum"
  lang csharp
  file crypto/src/math/ec/ECCurve.cs
  reject VYQL-DOS-006
  reject VYQL-DOS-042
  code
  ```
  public abstract class AbstractF2mCurve
      : ECCurve
  {
      private static IFiniteField BuildField(int m, int k1, int k2, int k3)
      {
          int maxM = ImplGetInteger("Org.BouncyCastle.EC.F2m_MaxSize", 1142); // 2 * 571
          if (m > maxM)
              throw new ArgumentException("F2m m value out of range");

          int[] exponents = (k2 | k3) == 0
              ? new int[]{ 0, k1, m }
              : new int[]{ 0, k1, k2, k3, m };

          return FiniteFields.GetBinaryExtensionField(exponents);
      }

      protected AbstractF2mCurve(int m, int k1, int k2, int k3)
          : base(BuildField(m, k1, k2, k3))
      {
      }
  }
  ```
